+# RabbitMQ Integration for Feedless
+
+This package provides comprehensive RabbitMQ integration for the Feedless application, enabling asynchronous message processing for various operations.
+
+## Overview
+
+The RabbitMQ integration includes:
+
+- **Message DTOs**: Structured message types for different operations
+- **Message Producer**: Service for sending messages to RabbitMQ queues
+- **Message Consumer**: Service for processing messages from RabbitMQ queues
+- **Configuration**: Complete RabbitMQ setup with exchanges, queues, and bindings
+- **High-level Service**: Convenient methods for common messaging patterns
+- **Test Support**: Testcontainer integration for integration testing
+
+## Architecture
+
+### Exchanges and Queues
+
+The system uses a topic exchange pattern with the following structure:
+
+- **Main Exchange**: `feedless.exchange`
+- **Dead Letter Exchange**: `feedless.dlx.exchange`
+
+#### Queues:
+1. **Feed Processing**: `feedless.feed.processing`
+2. **Document Processing**: `feedless.document.processing`
+3. **Notifications**: `feedless.notification`
+4. **Analytics**: `feedless.analytics`
+
+Each queue has a corresponding dead letter queue (DLQ) for handling failed messages.
+
+### Message Types
+
+#### FeedProcessingMessage
+Used for scheduling feed processing tasks.
+```kotlin
+FeedProcessingMessage(
+    feedId = "feed-123",
+    feedUrl = "https://example.com/feed.xml",
+    repositoryId = "repo-456",
+    priority = 5
+)
+```
+
+#### DocumentProcessingMessage
+Used for scheduling document processing tasks.
+```kotlin
+DocumentProcessingMessage(
+    documentId = "doc-789",
+    documentUrl = "https://example.com/document.pdf",
+    extractionType = "FULL",
+    priority = 3
+)
+```
+
+#### NotificationMessage
+Used for sending notifications to users.
+```kotlin
+NotificationMessage(
+    userId = "user-123",
+    type = "FEED_UPDATE",
+    title = "New articles available",
+    content = "Your subscribed feed has new articles",
+    metadata = mapOf("feedId" to "feed-123")
+)
+```
+
+#### AnalyticsMessage
+Used for tracking analytics events.
+```kotlin
+AnalyticsMessage(
+    eventType = "PAGE_VIEW",
+    userId = "user-456",
+    sessionId = "session-789",
+    properties = mapOf("page" to "/dashboard")
+)
+```
+
+## Usage
+
+### Sending Messages
+
+#### Using RabbitMessageProducer (Low-level)
+```kotlin
+@Autowired
+private lateinit var messageProducer: RabbitMessageProducer
+
+fun scheduleFeedProcessing() {
+    val message = FeedProcessingMessage(
+        feedId = "my-feed",
+        feedUrl = "https://example.com/feed.xml"
+    )
+    messageProducer.sendFeedProcessingMessage(message)
+}
+```
+
+#### Using RabbitMessageService (High-level, Recommended)
+```kotlin
+@Autowired
+private lateinit var messageService: RabbitMessageService
+
+fun scheduleFeeds() {
+    // Single feed
+    messageService.scheduleFeedProcessing("feed-1", "https://example.com/feed1.xml")
+    
+    // Urgent processing
+    messageService.scheduleUrgentFeedProcessing("urgent-feed", "https://urgent.com/feed.xml")
+    
+    // Batch processing
+    val feeds = listOf(
+        "feed-1" to "https://example.com/feed1.xml",
+        "feed-2" to "https://example.com/feed2.xml"
+    )
+    messageService.batchScheduleFeedProcessing(feeds)
+    
+    // Send notification
+    messageService.sendUserNotification(
+        userId = "user-123",
+        type = "INFO",
+        title = "Processing Complete",
+        content = "Your feeds have been processed"
+    )
+    
+    // Track analytics
+    messageService.trackUserActivity(
+        userId = "user-123",
+        sessionId = "session-456",
+        action = "FEED_SUBSCRIBE",
+        resource = "feed-789"
+    )
+}
+```
+
+### Processing Messages
+
+Message processing is handled automatically by the `RabbitMessageConsumer` service. The consumers are annotated with `@RabbitListener` and will automatically process messages as they arrive.
+
+#### Custom Message Processing
+
+To add custom processing logic, modify the appropriate method in `RabbitMessageConsumer`:
+
+```kotlin
+@RabbitListener(queues = [RabbitMQConfig.FEED_PROCESSING_QUEUE])
+fun processFeedMessage(message: FeedProcessingMessage, @Header("amqp_receivedRoutingKey") routingKey: String) {
+    try {
+        // Your custom feed processing logic here
+        feedService.processFeed(message.feedId, message.feedUrl)
+        log.info("Successfully processed feed: {}", message.feedId)
+    } catch (Exception e) {
+        log.error("Failed to process feed: {}", message.feedId, e)
+        throw e // Message will be moved to DLQ
+    }
+}
+```
+
+## Configuration
+
+### Application Properties
+
+#### Development (application-dev.properties)
+```properties
+spring.rabbitmq.host=localhost
+spring.rabbitmq.port=5672
+spring.rabbitmq.username=guest
+spring.rabbitmq.password=guest
+```
+
+#### Production (application-prod.properties)
+```properties
+spring.rabbitmq.host=${RABBITMQ_HOST:localhost}
+spring.rabbitmq.port=${RABBITMQ_PORT:5672}
+spring.rabbitmq.username=${RABBITMQ_USERNAME:guest}
+spring.rabbitmq.password=${RABBITMQ_PASSWORD:guest}
+spring.rabbitmq.virtual-host=${RABBITMQ_VIRTUAL_HOST:/}
+```
+
+### Docker Compose
+
+Add RabbitMQ to your docker-compose.yml:
+
+```yaml
+services:
+  rabbitmq:
+    image: rabbitmq:3.13-management-alpine
+    container_name: feedless-rabbitmq
+    ports:
+      - "5672:5672"
+      - "15672:15672"  # Management UI
+    environment:
+      RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER: feedless
+      RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS: password
+    volumes:
+      - rabbitmq_data:/var/lib/rabbitmq
+    healthcheck:
+      test: ["CMD", "rabbitmq-diagnostics", "ping"]
+      interval: 30s
+      timeout: 10s
+      retries: 3
+
+volumes:
+  rabbitmq_data:
+```
+
+## Testing
+
+### Integration Tests
+
+The project includes comprehensive integration tests using Testcontainers:
+
+```kotlin
+@SpringBootTest
+@ExtendWith(SpringJUnitExtension::class, RabbitMQExtension::class)
+@ActiveProfiles("test")
+class RabbitMQIntegrationTest {
+    
+    @Autowired
+    private lateinit var messageService: RabbitMessageService
+    
+    @Test
+    fun `should process feed message successfully`() {
+        messageService.scheduleFeedProcessing("test-feed", "https://example.com/feed.xml")
+        // Verify processing...
+    }
+}
+```
+
+### Running Tests
+
+```bash
+./gradlew test
+```
+
+## Monitoring and Management
+
+### RabbitMQ Management UI
+
+Access the management UI at `http://localhost:15672` (default credentials: guest/guest)
+
+### Key Metrics to Monitor
+
+1. **Queue Depth**: Number of messages waiting in queues
+2. **Message Rates**: Messages published/consumed per second
+3. **Dead Letter Queue Messages**: Failed messages requiring attention
+4. **Consumer Status**: Active consumers and their performance
+5. **Memory Usage**: RabbitMQ memory consumption
+
+### Health Checks
+
+The application includes health check endpoints for RabbitMQ:
+
+- `/actuator/health/rabbit` - RabbitMQ connection status
+
+## Error Handling
+
+### Retry Strategy
+
+Messages that fail processing are automatically retried based on configuration:
+
+```properties
+spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.enabled=true
+spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.max-attempts=3
+spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.initial-interval=2000
+spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.multiplier=2
+spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.max-interval=10000
+```
+
+### Dead Letter Queues
+
+Messages that exceed retry attempts are moved to dead letter queues for manual inspection and potential reprocessing.
+
+### Monitoring Failed Messages
+
+Check dead letter queues regularly:
+- `feedless.feed.processing.dlq`
+- `feedless.document.processing.dlq`
+- `feedless.notification.dlq`
+- `feedless.analytics.dlq`
+
+## Best Practices
+
+1. **Message Size**: Keep messages small and use references to large data
+2. **Idempotency**: Design message processors to be idempotent
+3. **Error Handling**: Always handle exceptions gracefully
+4. **Monitoring**: Monitor queue depths and processing rates
+5. **Testing**: Use testcontainers for integration testing
+6. **Security**: Use proper authentication in production
+7. **Persistence**: Use durable queues for important messages
+8. **Batching**: Consider batching for high-volume operations
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+### Common Issues
+
+1. **Connection Refused**: Check RabbitMQ server is running and accessible
+2. **Queue Not Found**: Ensure queues are created by configuration
+3. **Messages Not Processed**: Check consumer status and error logs
+4. **High Memory Usage**: Monitor message accumulation in queues
+5. **Slow Processing**: Check consumer concurrency settings
+
+### Debugging
+
+Enable debug logging for RabbitMQ:
+
+```properties
+logging.level.org.springframework.amqp=DEBUG
+logging.level.org.migor.feedless.message=DEBUG
+```
+
+## Future Enhancements
+
+Potential improvements to consider:
+
+1. **Message Prioritization**: Enhanced priority queue support
+2. **Delayed Messages**: Plugin support for scheduled message delivery
+3. **Message Compression**: Automatic compression for large messages
+4. **Metrics Integration**: Custom metrics for business logic
+5. **Circuit Breaker**: Fault tolerance for external dependencies
+6. **Message Routing**: Dynamic routing based on message content
+7. **Clustering**: Multi-node RabbitMQ setup for high availability
